# A request's cache_salt (vLLM's field) isolates its kept prompt states (number 0130; was 0117, renumbered after main
# took 0109-0129; rebased over 0120-glm-fork-point-shared, which moved the _keep lines this patch edits).
#
# vLLM's OpenAI server takes `cache_salt` on chat and completion requests: a request resumes only cached prefixes
# written under the same salt. Benchmarks rely on it - RigMark (github.com/alexellis/rigmark, protocol 1.2+) sends a
# fresh random salt with every cold / immediate-replay prefill pair, so reusing a comparison ID cannot warm the next
# sweep's cold sample, and fails the run when a cold sample reports cached tokens. TensorFold ignored the field and
# keyed kept states by token ids alone, so a second run of one comparison ID on a server that still held the first
# run's states (the same process, or the --spill-gib store across restarts) read the cold prompts as hits.
#
# 1. app.py: `cache_salt` must be a non-empty string of at most 256 characters when present (vLLM's rule plus a
#    bound, so a kept state carries a salt of a bounded size), refused before a stream opens (`_check_fields`);
#    the salt reaches the engine through `engine.request.cache_salt`, beside policy / stop_eos.
# 2. A kept state carries the salt of the request that kept it (`snap.salt`; rank 1 holds "" for a salted one: it
#    only needs to know there is one). `_resume` (parallel and serial) matches only states of the request's own salt;
#    None is unsalted, exactly as before. Shared-prefix points come only from its own salt's kept prompts. A state
#    kept with the same ids replaces an older one only when both are salted or neither is (`_keep`, `_remember`):
#    neither resumes the other, so a salted request no longer drops an unsalted conversation's state, nor the other
#    way round (two salts of the same ids still replace each other: rank 1 knows only that a state is salted).
# 3. A salted request reads nothing from the spill store (`prestage`, `find`) and a salted state is never written to
#    it (`_unspillable` / `_unwritable` "salted"): the store keys entries by token ids, which cannot tell salts apart.
# 4. The salted flag goes to rank 1 as the ADMIT's last value (parallel) or the serial header's last value, so both
#    ranks decide spill writes and replacements alike, and rank 1's serial resume takes the state as salted as rank 0's.
# 5. The translated routes (round 2): a /v1/responses request's `cache_salt` passes to the chat completion that runs
#    it (`responses_translate.PASSED`) instead of being dropped, and /v1/messages - whose protocol has no such field -
#    refuses it (`anthropic_translate.translate`) rather than running the request silently unsalted.
# Unsalted requests are unchanged; replies are unchanged (a salt only turns a resume into a fresh prefill of the same
# ids). CPU check: tools/cache_salt_check.py.
